    ],
    bottomLine: "The bar to choose if you want to pick bushings or bearings without changing anything else. BarBend names it its best bar for CrossFit.",
    description: "Fringe Sport's Wonder Bar V2 is a multi-purpose bar sold in two versions that differ only in the sleeves: bronze oil-impregnated bushings for a moderate spin, or four needle-bearing cartridges per sleeve for a fast one. Fringe lists it at 20 kg and 86 inches, with a 28 mm shaft, 16-inch sleeves, dual knurl marks with no center knurl, a stiff whip, a black zinc finish, a 1,600 lb weight capacity and a lifetime warranty against manufacturer defects. BarBend gives the same capacity, diameter and warranty and calls it its best barbell for CrossFit; Garage Gym Reviews gives it the same title in its own guide. The tensile strength is the problem figure. Fringe's specification list says 205,000 PSI, which both review sites repeat, but Fringe's own comparison table on the same page says 202,000 PSI, so we have not put either number on the card. Either way it sits at the top of the range on this page, and Fringe also lists a 160,000 PSI yield strength. On 19 October 2026 the bushing bar cost $299 and the bearing bar $319. BarBend's criticisms are cosmetic: sleeves that scar quickly and a black zinc finish that fades. A 15 kg version is sold too.",
  },
  {
    id: "rep-colorado-bar-15kg",
    name: "REP Fitness Colorado Bar (15 kg)",
    category: "15 kg multi-purpose bar with a 25 mm shaft",
    badge: "Best 15 kg Bar",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=15kg+olympic+barbell&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $270",
    priceBounds: {"low":270,"high":270},
    specs: "15 kg · 25 mm shaft · 79.1 in. long · 12.4 in. loadable sleeves · 190,000 PSI (maker) · composite bushings · dual marks · lifetime warranty",
    pros: [
      "A 25 mm shaft that is easier to grip for smaller hands",
      "The same 190,000 PSI rating and composite bushings as the 20 kg Colorado",
      "At 79.1 inches it is shorter than a 7 ft bar, which helps in tight rooms",
      "Lifetime warranty",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Shorter 12.4-inch sleeves hold fewer bumper plates",
      "Fewer colour choices than the 20 kg bar",
      "Not found on Amazon US",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The 15 kg bar to buy for a thinner grip, a lighter start or a second bar for the household.",
    description: "REP's 15 kg Colorado is the women's-standard version of the bar above, and Garage Gym Reviews names it its best multi-purpose bar for women. REP lists it at 15 kg (33.1 lb) and 79.1 inches long, with a 25 mm shaft, 12.4-inch loadable sleeves, composite bushings, a medium volcano knurl with dual marks, no center knurl, a medium whip and a 190,000 PSI tensile strength; Garage Gym Reviews gives the same shaft, strength and bushings. REP rates it at the same 1,500 lb static load as the 20 kg bar and lists a lifetime warranty. It comes in hard chrome or in pink or black Cerakote with Duracoat sleeves, and REP charged $269.99 for all three on 19 October 2026. Garage Gym Reviews' criticisms are the limited colours and, at the time of its review, few customer reviews. The real limit is the sleeves: 12.4 inches is plenty for most training but holds fewer thick bumper plates than a 20 kg bar's 16 inches. Before ordering, check that the bar's shaft will span your rack's J-cups, as explained in the rack-fit section above. Fringe Sport's 15 kg Wonder Bar, listed at 160,000 PSI and $239 to $279, is the cheaper alternative.",
  },
  {
    id: "titan-performance-series-olympic-barbell",
    name: "Titan Fitness Performance Series Olympic Barbell",
    category: "Budget 20 kg multi-purpose bar",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=olympic+barbell&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$168–$210",
    priceBounds: {"low":168,"high":210},
    specs: "20 kg · 28 mm shaft · 165,000 PSI (maker) · bronze bushings · 700 lb capacity (maker) · medium volcano knurl, dual marks · 1-year warranty",
    pros: [
      "165,000 PSI steel, well above the cheapest bars, for under $210",
      "Bronze bushings and a medium volcano knurl suit general training",
      "Titan lists 51.5 inches between the sleeves, useful when checking rack fit",
      "Dual knurl marks for both pressing and Olympic-lift grips",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 700 lb rating is low for heavy lifters",
      "Only a 1-year warranty, against lifetime cover on the bars above",
      "We could not confirm a current Amazon US listing for this exact bar",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A sensible first bar on a tight budget for presses, rows, squats and moderate deadlifts. Spend more if you lift heavy or want a long warranty.",
    description: "Titan's Performance Series Olympic Barbell is the budget pick for buyers who want more than the cheapest bars offer without paying for a 190,000 PSI bar. Titan lists it at 20 kg and 86.6 inches long, with a 28 mm shaft, 16.25-inch loadable sleeves, 51.5 inches between the sleeves, bronze bushings, a medium volcano knurl with dual marks and no center knurl, a chrome finish on both shaft and sleeves, a 165,000 PSI tensile strength and a 700 lb weight capacity. Torokhtiy's independent review gives the same strength, capacity, shaft and bushings, rates the grip highly and recommends it for beginner and intermediate lifters. On 19 October 2026 Titan's store showed it at $167.97, reduced from $209.99, with a one-year warranty. The weak points follow from the price: 700 lb is the lowest rating on this page apart from the unrated figures on the CAP, and a one-year warranty is short for something that should last decades. Titan's separate Performance Series Power Barbell is a different, 29 mm bar; make sure you order the Olympic version. Titan sells widely on Amazon, but we could not confirm this exact bar there on the day.",
  },
  {
    id: "cap-beast-olympic-bar",
    name: "CAP Barbell \"The Beast\" 7 ft Olympic Bar",
    category: "Budget 7 ft Olympic bar sold on Amazon",
    badge: "Cheapest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=CAP+Barbell+The+Beast+7ft+Olympic+Bar+OB-86B&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$133–$150",
    priceBounds: {"low":133,"high":150},
    specs: "20 kg · 7 ft · 110,000 PSI (Amazon listing, BarBend and Garage Gym Reviews) · bushings · black oxide shaft, polished sleeves · no center knurl",
    pros: [
      "Long sold on Amazon, with Prime delivery and returns",
      "Fits every Olympic plate and every full-size rack built for a 7 ft bar",
      "Medium-depth diamond knurl and a plain black oxide finish",
      "The cheapest bar here that its reviewers still call usable",
    ],
    cons: [
      "110,000 PSI is far below the other bars, leaving much less margin before it bends",
      "Its weight rating and shaft diameter differ between CAP, Amazon and the reviewers",
      "CAP's own store showed it sold out on the day we checked",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Only for light to moderate training on a strict budget. If you can stretch to the Titan or the Synergee, do.",
    description: "CAP's Beast — model OBIS-86B, often listed as the OB-86B — has been one of the most common Olympic bars on Amazon for years, and BarBend names it the best barbell on Amazon. It is a 20 kg, 7 ft bar with bushing sleeves, a medium-depth diamond knurl, a black oxide shaft and polished sleeves. Its tensile strength is 110,000 PSI according to its Amazon listing, BarBend and Garage Gym Reviews; CAP's own page does not give one. That figure is the reason to hesitate: Garage Gym Reviews dropped the bar from its recommendations after CAP lowered the steel's rating, and every other bar on this page is rated at least 50% higher. The other numbers disagree. CAP's page rates it at 1,000 lb and the Amazon listing at 1,200 lb; Garage Gym Reviews gives a 28.5 mm shaft and BarBend 30 mm; and only Garage Gym Reviews mentions a warranty. None of those is on the card. CAP's store listed it at $149.99 but sold out on 19 October 2026, and BarBend saw $133.49 on Amazon earlier in the year. It is a reasonable bar for presses, rows and moderate squats. For heavy pulls, regular drops or a bar you want to keep for decades, the Titan or Synergee is a better use of the money.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Home Gym", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/home-gym/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Barbells", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-barbells/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best barbell for a home gym?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most people, a 20 kg multi-purpose bar with dual knurl marks, bushing sleeves and a tensile strength around 190,000 PSI, such as the REP Colorado Bar at about $300. The Synergee Games bar has the same headline ratings for less and is sold on Amazon; the Rogue Ohio Bar is the made-in-USA alternative.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good tensile strength for a barbell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Around 190,000 PSI is the norm for good multi-purpose bars, and 165,000 PSI is a reasonable minimum for anyone who will lift heavy or drop the bar with bumpers. Bars rated near 110,000 PSI or lower are fine for light work but bend more easily. These are makers' claims, not independent tests.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are bearings or bushings better on a barbell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Neither is better overall. Bushings spin more slowly and steadily, need little care and suit squats, presses, deadlifts and general training. Needle bearings spin faster and suit cleans and snatches. For a single home gym bar, bushings are the safer default unless Olympic weightlifting is your main training.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I buy a 20 kg or 15 kg barbell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most people should start with a 20 kg bar, because it is the standard for racks, programmes and sleeve space. A 15 kg bar has a thinner 25 mm shaft and is shorter, which helps smaller hands and tight rooms, but it holds fewer plates and may not span every rack. Check rack fit first.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a 6 ft barbell fit my squat rack?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Only if the smooth section between the sleeve collars is longer than the outside width of your rack at the J-cups. Full-size racks are designed around 7 ft bars, whose shaft between the collars is about 51.5 to 52 inches. Measure your rack and compare it with the short bar maker's figure before ordering.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is an Olympic barbell the same as a standard barbell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Olympic bars have 2-inch (50 mm) rotating sleeves and take plates with 2-inch holes. Standard bars have 1-inch ends, usually do not rotate and take 1-inch plates. The two types of plate are not interchangeable, and standard bars are generally rated for much lighter loads.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-19",
      dateModified: "2026-10-19",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 7,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-barbells/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-19",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-19",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "best barbell",
      "best olympic barbell",
      "barbell for home gym",
      "olympic barbell 20kg",
      "womens 15kg barbell",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See Our Top Picks", link: "#picks" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-bestbarbells" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-bestbarbells)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buying Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Best Barbell <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">for a Home Gym: 7 Olympic Bars Compared</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          The barbell is the one piece of a home gym you touch on every lift, and most of what separates a good one from a poor one is printed on the spec sheet: tensile strength, sleeve rotation, knurl, shaft diameter and finish. Here are seven currently sold Olympic bars from about $135 to about $365, what each is honestly good for, and how to tell whether a bar will fit your rack and your room.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#picks" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            See the Picks
          </a>
          <a href="#rack-fit" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Will It Fit Your Rack?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 19, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          barbells, and no figure on this page is our own measurement. On 19 October 2026 we
          confirmed each bar as currently listed on its maker's own store (REP Fitness, Synergee,
          Rogue Fitness, Fringe Sport, Titan Fitness and CAP Barbell) and checked its weight, shaft
          diameter, tensile strength, sleeve rotation, knurl and warranty against at least one
          independent source: Garage Gym Reviews' Olympic, budget and women's barbell guides and its
          equipment page for the CAP bar, BarBend's barbell guide, and an independent review of the
          Titan bar. A figure appears on a card only where the maker and an independent source agree;
          where they conflicted — on one bar's tensile strength, another's weight rating and shaft
          diameter, and one bar's overall length — we left it out and say so on the card. Tensile
          strength and weight ratings are the makers' own claims, not independent tests.
          <strong class="text-gray-900">Where to buy.</strong> Amazon's pages would not load for us
          that day, so Amazon availability was confirmed from Amazon US listings returned by search:
          we found the Synergee Games bar and CAP's Beast bar listed there. We could not find REP's
          Colorado bars, the Fringe Sport Wonder Bar or Titan's Performance Series bar on Amazon US;
          REP, Fringe and Titan sell them on their own stores, and Rogue sells only through its own
          store, so those buttons run an Amazon search that may show similar bars rather than the
